<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augustinas Voldemaras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augustinas Voldemaras was a Lithuanian historian and politician who became the first prime minister of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in November 1918. After the military coup of December 1926 he again became prime minister, ruling alongside President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a>, until Smetona dismissed him in 1929. His supporters in the Iron Wolf movement later attempted coups; he was deported by the Soviets in 1940 and died in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> prison in 1942.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustinas-voldemaras.jpg" alt="Augustinas Voldemaras" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augustinas_Voldemaras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Augustinas Voldemaras was born on 16 April 1883 in the village of Dysna, then part of the Vilna Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, to a family of modest means. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1890 and in 1901 met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a>, who became his close friend, political collaborator and eventually his political rival. He graduated in history and philosophy at the Saint Petersburg Imperial University in 1909 and later became a professor at its branch in Perm. He knew sixteen languages and wrote academic works in Lithuanian and French.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1918 Voldemaras joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> and, with Smetona, helped unite Lithuanian organisations behind it. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat and recognition of Lithuanian independence, the Council's Presidium chose him as the first prime minister. He took office on 11 November 1918 and kept the foreign and defence ministries for himself. On 21 December he left for Germany and the Versailles Peace Conference, which caused outrage, as he was seen as fleeing the Soviet advance. Mykolas Sleževičius replaced him on 26 December, but he stayed foreign minister until June 1920 and found little support at Versailles.</p><p>He was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seimas">Seimas</a> in 1926. With Smetona, as leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-nationalist-union">Lithuanian Nationalist Union</a>, he supported the military coup of 17 December 1926 against President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazys-grinius">Kazys Grinius</a> and became prime minister again on condition that he uphold the constitution of 1922. Within a year the Seimas was dissolved without new elections, and in 1928 a new constitution replaced the old. In January 1928 he signed a border treaty in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> accepted Lithuanian possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a>. He also helped establish the nationalist Iron Wolf organisation. After an attempt on his life on 6 May 1929 Smetona removed him from office on 19 September 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Voldemaras was exiled to Plateliai and later Zarasai. In June 1934 officers linked to the Iron Wolf attempted a coup against Smetona with Voldemaras's involvement, but it failed, and he was imprisoned for four years until an amnesty on condition that he leave the country. He chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In June 1940, days after the Soviet occupation, he was arrested at the border and imprisoned, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s Butyrka prison in 1942. His burial place is unknown, and a memorial stone was unveiled in Petrašiūnai cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augustinas Voldemaras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Germany’s defeat, the first government under <b>Augustinas Voldemaras</b> was formed in November 1918, and Smetona became the first president in April 1919.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Germany's defeat in November 1918 the Lithuanians formed their first government under Prime Minister <b>Augustinas Voldemaras</b> and adopted a provisional constitution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dysna, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in Butyrka prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.32,26.78&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Dysna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5136 275 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Augustinas Voldemaras"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5586 545h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5586 545h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.32,26.78&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
